# Track 4 — E2E round 3: 40 vCPU total budget
#
# NOTE (2026-06): tried bumping issuer 4 -> 6 to lift R3, but it LOWERED
# throughput (424 -> 350) because it starved the request-handler. A docker
# stats capture at 400 VU then showed NOTHING on the manager pinned (handler
# ~60% of cap, the 6 issuers only ~23% of cap, everything else idle). So R3's
# ceiling is NOT the manager overhead — it's the worker-side verifier pool +
# the weighted selector keeping load on the cheap verifiers (cost stuck ~1.1).
# Manager rebalancing can't move it. Reverted to the best-measured config.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | proof-queue Redis    | 0.55 x 1        | 0.55     |
# | snark+token Redis    | 0.15 x 2        | 0.3      |
# | request-handler      | 0.8 x 4         | 3.2      |
# | verifier-selector    | 0.7 x 1         | 0.7      |
# | token-issuer         | 1.0 x 4         | 4.0      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 30        | 30.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.25 x 5        | 1.25     |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 40.0     |
#
# R3 lands ~424/s (knee ~250-300 VU). That's its real number — pipeline/routing
# bound, like a mid-budget service deployment.
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round3.env; set +a
PROOF_CPUS=0.55
REDIS_CPUS=0.15
HANDLER_REPLICAS=4
HANDLER_CPUS=0.8
SELECTOR_CPUS=0.7
ISSUER_REPLICAS=4
ISSUER_CPUS=1.0
SNARK_REPLICAS=30
SNARK_COUNT=30
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.25
